= What Controlling Actually Is

The plan promised 10,000 units at ₹50 each, delivered in 48 hours. The month
ends. Now comes the question that keeps management honest: *did we actually do
what we said we would?* Controlling answers it — systematically. It measures
what really happened, holds it against the standard that planning fixed,
finds the gap, and does something about the gap. Without this loop, planning
is a wish list and performance drifts wherever it likes.

- *Definition:* controlling means *comparing actual performance with the
  planned standards*, finding out the *deviations*, and taking *corrective
  action* so that goals are achieved.
- *A managerial function at every level:* every manager controls the work
  under them — the supervisor checks the day's output, the CEO checks the
  year's results.
- *Not bossing:* controlling measures performance *against standards* — it is
  about goals and numbers, not scolding people.

== Why controlling matters — the importance

- *Accomplishing goals:* the control loop keeps efforts pointed at the plan —
  deviations get caught early, and the goal survives the journey.
- *Judging the accuracy of standards:* experience of controlling also tests
  the standards themselves — unrealistic targets are exposed and improved.
- *Making efficient use of resources:* by tracking cost, time and quality
  against benchmarks, control exposes waste and directs resources where they
  pay.
- *Improving motivation:* employees who know performance is measured — and
  rewarded against standards — work with more care and energy.
- *Ensuring order and discipline:* honest reporting against standards creates
  a culture of accountability; discipline follows naturally.
- *Facilitating coordination:* standards and measurement knit departments
  together — each one's targets and results fit into the whole.

= Planning and Controlling — Inseparable Twins

The syllabus singles out this relationship because it is the conceptual heart
of the unit — and questions return to it year after year. The two functions
are *not two separate activities*: they are the two halves of one cycle.

- *Planning is the basis of controlling:* every comparison in controlling is
  made against a *standard that planning set* — the target, the budget, the
  timeline. Take the plan away and control has nothing to measure against.
- *Controlling completes planning:* the deviations and lessons found by
  controlling feed the *next round of planning* — better standards, better
  premises, better choices.
- *Both look backward and forward:* planning is *forward-looking* (it prepares
  for the future) and controlling is *backward-looking* (it compares what
  happened) — yet controlling's corrections shape the future, and planning's
  standards shape what will be measured. Each reaches into the other's time.
- *The loop line to memorise:* *planning without controlling is blind;
  controlling without planning is meaningless.*

