= The Control Process — Four Steps

#figure(image("assets/bs_controlling_process.png", width: 100%), caption: [Figure 1 — The control process: four steps circling from standards to corrective action, with critical point control and management by exception inside step four.])

- *Step 1 — Setting performance standards:* fix the benchmarks against which
  performance will be judged — in *units, cost, time or quality*. Standards
  may be *quantitative* (10,000 units, ₹5 lakh, 48 hours) or *qualitative*
  (courtesy of staff, quality of service), in which case firms convert them
  into measurable proxies — complaint counts, survey scores.
- *Step 2 — Measuring actual performance:* collect real results through
  samples, reports and inspections — *accurately, objectively and on time*,
  because a wrong measurement poisons every later step.
- *Step 3 — Comparing performance with standards:* put actual beside standard
  and size the gap — which targets were met, which missed, and by how much.
  Percentage deviations, cost gaps and unit shortfalls all surface here.
- *Step 4 — Analysing deviations and taking corrective action:* not every gap
  deserves equal attention — *critical point control* keeps watch on the key
  result areas, and *management by exception* escalates only significant
  deviations to higher levels. Then act: fix the process, revise an unrealistic
  standard, or retrain and reassign the team.

