= Common Errors and Special Cases

#exception-box(title: "Common Errors & Special Cases")[
  #table(
    columns: (1.35fr, 1.35fr, 1.2fr),
    table.header[*The mistake students make*][*What is right*][*The why / the trap*],
    ["Controlling is the last function, so it ends the cycle"], [It closes *and restarts* the cycle — corrective action becomes the next plan's input], [The process diagram is a circle; the exam rewards seeing the loop, not the line.],
    ["Controlling means scolding employees for mistakes"], [Controlling means *measuring against standards* and correcting the *process*], [Standards, not tempers — discipline is one by-product, not the definition.],
    ["Any deviation must be reported to top management"], [*Management by exception* — only significant deviations escalate; small ones are handled below], [Escalating everything buries the top in noise and delays the big calls.],
    ["Controlling can exist without planning"], [Control without planning is *meaningless* — there are no standards to compare with], [The twinship is the unit's heart; expect at least one question on it.],
    ["Qualitative standards cannot be measured at all"], [They are converted into *measurable proxies* — complaint counts, survey scores, return rates], ["Courtesy of staff" becomes an average service score — measurable.],
    ["Measuring performance decides everything"], [A wrong or late *measurement* poisons comparison and action — measurement must be accurate, objective, timely], [Garbage in, garbage out — step 2 protects every later step.],
  )
]

- *Spot the pattern:* the traps hide in *direction* — who supplies what, which
  way the loop runs, what escalates and what does not. Draw the circle and the
  answers follow.
- *One special case:* when a question says "only deviations above 10% are
  reported to the general manager" — the named idea is *management by
  exception*, part of step four.

