= Practice Questions

#mcq(1, [Controlling as a function of management means:], (
  [ordering employees to work harder], [comparing actual performance with planned standards and taking corrective action on deviations], [preparing budgets for the next year], [recruiting the right employees for each job],
))

#mcq(2, [Why is controlling called a backward-looking as well as a forward-looking function?], (
  [it compares past performance and its corrections shape future plans], [it only reviews the past], [it only prepares for the future], [it neither looks backward nor forward],
))

#mcq(3, [The standards against which controlling measures performance are supplied by:], (
  [organising], [staffing], [planning], [directing],
))

#mcq(4, [The first step of the control process is:], (
  [measuring actual performance], [setting performance standards], [comparing performance with standards], [analysing deviations],
))

#mcq(5, ["Only deviations of more than 10% from the standard are reported to top management; smaller gaps are settled at lower levels." The idea described is:], (
  [critical point control], [management by exception], [job enrichment], [span of management],
))

#mcq(6, [A factory tracks its production bottleneck and machine downtime most closely, since these decide the whole plant's output, and watches them more than routine figures. The idea is:], (
  [critical point control], [management by exception], [quantitative standards], [corrective action],
))

#mcq(7, [Which of the following is a *qualitative* standard?], (
  [produce 10,000 units per month], [keep production cost at ₹50 per unit], [deliver orders within 48 hours], [maintain courtesy of staff toward customers],
))

#mcq(8, ["Planning is meaningless without controlling and controlling is blind without planning." The relationship shown is:], (
  [both are separate and independent functions], [planning supplies the standards and controlling feeds back the lessons — one continuous loop], [controlling replaces planning after implementation], [planning is done by top level, controlling by supervisory level only],
))

#mcq(9, [Actual output was 9,000 units against a standard of 10,000. The manager finds a machine fault and repairs it to prevent a repeat. Which step of the control process does the repair belong to?], (
  [setting performance standards], [measuring actual performance], [comparing with standards], [taking corrective action],
))

#mcq(10, [Which importance point of controlling is served when tracked cost and time figures expose waste and redirect resources to their best use?], (
  [judging the accuracy of standards], [efficient use of resources], [ensuring order and discipline], [improving motivation],
))

= Solutions

#solution(1, [Controlling is the *measuring* function: actual performance is compared with the *planned standards*, deviations are found and *corrective action* follows. Ordering employees is directing; budgets belong to planning.], answer: "B")

#solution(2, [Controlling looks *backward* — it reviews performance already achieved against standards — and *forward*, because its corrections and lessons shape the next plan. Both directions together keep the cycle alive.], answer: "A")

#solution(3, [Every comparison needs a benchmark, and benchmarks are the *targets, budgets and timelines that planning set*. Planning is the basis of controlling — without it, control has nothing to measure against.], answer: "C")

#solution(4, [The cycle opens by *setting performance standards* — the benchmarks in units, cost, time or quality. Measuring comes second, comparing third, and corrective action last.], answer: "B")

#solution(5, [Escalating only *significant* deviations while small gaps are settled below is *management by exception* — it protects top management's time for the deviations that truly matter.], answer: "B")

#solution(6, [Concentrating attention on the *key result areas* — the factors that decide overall output — is *critical point control*. Management by exception is about the *size* of deviations; this is about the *importance* of the point watched.], answer: "A")

#solution(7, [Courtesy of staff expresses a *quality of behaviour*, not a number — a *qualitative* standard, usually converted into proxies like survey scores or complaint counts. The other three are stated in figures.], answer: "D")

#solution(8, [Planning supplies the *standards*; controlling supplies the *feedback* that improves the next plan — the two form one continuous loop. Neither replaces the other, and both happen at every level.], answer: "B")

#solution(9, [Finding and fixing the *cause* of the deviation — repairing the machine — is *corrective action*, the final step. Measuring happened when the shortfall was counted; comparing happened when the gap was noticed.], answer: "D")

#solution(10, [Tracked figures that *expose waste and redirect resources* demonstrate the *efficient use of resources* — one of the six importance points of controlling.], answer: "B")

